body:
A downtime alert has to do two things: say clearly what stopped, and capture a reaction (acknowledge with an ETA, or escalate). This card is built for the Teams action 'Post adaptive card and wait for a response' of Power Automate (operation PostCardAndWaitForResponse), which pauses the flow until a user responds. That pattern uses Action.Submit buttons. All names, times and URLs in the JSON are invented examples. The card informs people; it does not stop a machine and it does not replace the physical stop or your emergency procedure.

## Card JSON

```json
{
  "type": "AdaptiveCard",
  "$schema": "http://adaptivecards.io/schemas/adaptive-card.json",
  "version": "1.5",
  "msteams": {
    "width": "Full"
  },
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "Container",
      "style": "attention",
      "bleed": true,
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "TextBlock",
          "text": "Machine stopped: CNC-07",
          "wrap": true,
          "size": "Large",
          "weight": "Bolder"
        },
        {
          "type": "TextBlock",
          "text": "Reason group: Electrical. Safety-relevant: Yes",
          "wrap": true,
          "isSubtle": true,
          "spacing": "None"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "FactSet",
      "facts": [
        {
          "title": "Area",
          "value": "Machining hall 2"
        },
        {
          "title": "Stopped at",
          "value": "2026-11-20 09:40"
        },
        {
          "title": "Reported by",
          "value": "Cell lead (role, not a name)"
        },
        {
          "title": "Planner task due",
          "value": "2026-11-20 11:40"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "Spindle drive fault, smell of burning. Machine is switched off at the main switch by the operator.",
      "wrap": true,
      "spacing": "Medium"
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.ChoiceSet",
      "id": "eta",
      "label": "When can you be at the machine?",
      "isRequired": true,
      "errorMessage": "Pick one option",
      "style": "compact",
      "value": "15",
      "choices": [
        {
          "title": "Within 15 minutes",
          "value": "15"
        },
        {
          "title": "Within 30 minutes",
          "value": "30"
        },
        {
          "title": "Within 60 minutes",
          "value": "60"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Input.Text",
      "id": "note",
      "label": "Note for the cell lead (optional)",
      "isMultiline": true,
      "maxLength": 300
    }
  ],
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "Action.Submit",
      "title": "I am on my way",
      "data": {
        "decision": "ack"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "Action.Submit",
      "title": "Cannot come, escalate",
      "data": {
        "decision": "escalate"
      },
      "associatedInputs": "none"
    },
    {
      "type": "Action.OpenUrl",
      "title": "Open downtime log item",
      "url": "https://contoso.example/sites/maintenance/Lists/DowntimeLog/DispForm.aspx?ID=1042"
    }
  ]
}
```

## How to use

1. In the flow, after the downtime item is created, add Teams 'Post adaptive card and wait for a response'. Post as Flow bot, Post in Chat with Flow bot (or a channel), and paste the card JSON into the card input. Replace the example values with dynamic content: machine, reason, start time, task due time and the item link.
2. Set the action timeout in the action settings (for example PT5M) and use 'Configure run after' with 'has timed out' for the escalation branch. Check that your action offers the timeout setting.
3. Read the response in the next action. The output is dynamic: open a test run and look at the data object. The input ids of this card are eta and note, and the button data is decision with the value ack or escalate. Use these in a Condition, never the button title text.
4. Update the downtime item (Status, acknowledgement time, ETA) and post a short confirmation in the channel.

## Worked example (invented data)

The flow posts the card to the maintenance lead's chat at 09:41. The lead chooses 'Within 15 minutes' and presses 'I am on my way'. The next action receives eta 15 and decision ack, writes AckAt 09:44 and ETA 15 into the item, and posts 'Maintenance acknowledged, ETA 15 min' into the channel. If nobody responds within five minutes, the timeout branch sends the same card to the second level.

## Common mistakes

- Putting Action.Execute into a card for the wait-for-response action. The bot style Action.Execute needs a bot handler; this flow action is designed around Action.Submit. Check what your tenant renders before you change it.
- Using isRequired on an input and then a 'cannot come' button that should work without answers. The 'escalate' button above sets associatedInputs to none, so validation is skipped for it.
- Reading the button by its title. Titles change when somebody translates the card; read data.decision.
- Putting names of operators into the card text. Use the role or the machine.
- Relying on a red header colour for urgency. The word 'stopped' and the safety line say it in text.

## Teams rendering limits and compatibility

- Teams rendering (Microsoft Learn cards reference, read 20 Nov 2026): bots and action-based message extensions support schema 1.6 or earlier, and the Teams mobile app up to 1.6; newer versions may render inconsistently. This card declares 1.5 on purpose, so it stays inside what older clients show.
- Adaptive Cards are not supported in tabs or connectors, and file or image uploads are not supported inside cards. Images must be PNG, JPEG or GIF up to 1024 x 1024 pixels, hosted at a public address without redirects; SVG and animated GIF are not supported.
- Design guidance from the same source: set wrap to true on text, avoid horizontal scrolling, use at most three columns per ColumnSet, give an explicit pixel width to at most one column and keep it small. Teams mobile does not scroll sideways.
- A card can have up to six actions, but one to three primary actions read best. The Learn page also lists positive and destructive action styling as not supported in some contexts, so the button titles must carry the meaning (check what your clients show).
- The whole message, including the card, is limited to about 28 KB (Teams connector page); keep cards small.
- The Container style attention colours the header band. If your theme or client shows it differently, the text still carries the message.
- An Action.OpenUrl button opens the item in a browser; the user must have access to the SharePoint site.

## Adapt it

Change the ETA choices, add a choice for 'Need spare parts' with a second input, or add a column of ids that map to your downtime reason codes. Keep the card short: the person reading it is walking to a machine.

## Privacy and security

Do not put secrets, links with tokens or personal data beyond a role into the card; cards stay in chat history. Agree with the works council that response times of named leads are not used for ranking.

An approval card for a nonconformance report should show the facts needed for a decision, collect a comment, and update after the click so a second approver cannot decide again. Universal Actions (Action.Execute, schema 1.4 and later) send a verb and the input values to your bot, which answers with a replacement card. The values in the JSON are invented examples. The card does not set who may approve which disposition. That authority comes from your quality procedures, and the bot has to check it on the server.

## Card JSON

