## How to use

1. Send the card from a Teams bot as an attachment with content type application/vnd.microsoft.card.adaptive, or test the layout first in the Adaptive Cards designer.
2. Handle the invoke activity adaptiveCard/action. Read the verb (ncrApprove, ncrReject, refreshNcr), the data (ncrId) and the inputs (disposition, comment) from the action data. Answer with HTTP 200 and a card without buttons, for example 'Approved by [role] at 14:05, comment saved'.
3. Look up the NCR on the server by ncrId. Ignore any text of the card that came back from the client; check that the user is allowed to approve this severity. Write the decision and the comment to the NCR history.
4. For a Reject without comment, answer with the same card plus a red TextBlock 'A comment is required'. Do not use isRequired on the comment input, because it would also block Approve.

## Worked example (invented data)

Quality engineer opens the card, taps 'Show inspection detail' (the Container with id detailBlock appears), selects 'Agree with proposed disposition' and presses Approve with the comment 'Rework per approved route, re-inspect 100 percent'. The bot checks that the user is the assigned approver, stores the decision with the time, and replaces the card with a read-only summary. If the user selects 'Need more information' and presses Reject, the bot asks for a comment first.

## Common mistakes

- Mixing Action.Submit and Action.Execute in one card. Pick Action.Execute for the whole card.
- Trusting the card's own facts when they come back. Always reload the NCR by id.
- Using the refresh block with userIds that are not in the format your bot gets. The placeholder id above is only a shape; use real ids from your bot.
- Putting part drawings, customer data or measurement files into the card. Link to the controlled system instead.
- Hiding information behind ToggleVisibility that the approver needs for every decision. Detail can be hidden; the proposed disposition must stay visible.

## Teams rendering limits and compatibility

- Teams rendering (Microsoft Learn cards reference, read 20 Nov 2026): bots and action-based message extensions support schema 1.6 or earlier, and the Teams mobile app up to 1.6; newer versions may render inconsistently. This card declares 1.5 on purpose, so it stays inside what older clients show.
- Adaptive Cards are not supported in tabs or connectors, and file or image uploads are not supported inside cards. Images must be PNG, JPEG or GIF up to 1024 x 1024 pixels, hosted at a public address without redirects; SVG and animated GIF are not supported.
- Design guidance from the same source: set wrap to true on text, avoid horizontal scrolling, use at most three columns per ColumnSet, give an explicit pixel width to at most one column and keep it small. Teams mobile does not scroll sideways.
- A card can have up to six actions, but one to three primary actions read best. The Learn page also lists positive and destructive action styling as not supported in some contexts, so the button titles must carry the meaning (check what your clients show).
- The whole message, including the card, is limited to about 28 KB (Teams connector page); keep cards small.
- Action.Execute needs schema 1.4 or higher and a handler in your bot. In a Workflows or Power Automate post action without a bot the click has no handler; use the Submit variant of the card there (check what your action supports).
- Action.ToggleVisibility and Action.OpenUrl are supported in Teams. The isEnabled property is not supported in Teams according to the Learn page, so do not rely on it to disable buttons.

## Adapt it

Replace the facts with your NCR fields, add a second input for 'Customer notification needed' that your bot only records, and add a row for the next approver in a multi-step chain. Keep the same verbs in code and card.

## Privacy and security

The card is stored in chat history. Use the NCR number and a short text, not names of inspectors or customer part drawings. Do not put tokens in the OpenUrl link. Decisions belong in the quality record, not only in the chat.

Safety observation programmes work when reporting is quick and safe for the reporter. This card collects the minimum: category, area, what was seen, what was done and whether anyone is in danger now. It does not take the place of the emergency procedure; if someone is in danger, people call the emergency number first. The card text says so. The example URL and the choices are invented; replace them with your categories, areas and links.

