## How to use

1. Post the card in a channel or in a bot chat, or make it available as a Teams message extension. For a bot, handle the Submit as a message with value; for Power Automate use 'Post adaptive card and wait for a response' for a single recipient and a bot for many.
2. Write one row to a 'Safety Observations' list with category, area, text, urgency and a flag whether contact is allowed. Store the reporter's name only when the toggle named is yes and your works council agreed. Otherwise store no identity.
3. If urgency is yes, notify the safety contact at once (Teams message and phone list). Do not wait for a daily summary.
4. Send the reporter a short confirmation in the chat: 'Received. The safety officer reviews new entries on [your cadence]'.

## Worked example (invented data)

A warehouse employee selects 'Unsafe condition', area 'Warehouse', writes 'Pallet racking aisle 4: bent upright at the bottom beam, forklift traffic', the immediate action 'Aisle taped off', urgency 'No', and leaves the contact toggle off. The flow stores the row without identity and posts it to the safety officer's queue. The officer reviews it, closes the aisle properly, and the status changes to Closed. If the urgency had been 'yes', a Teams message and the phone chain would start immediately.

## Common mistakes

- Collecting names by default. People report less when they fear blame; make identity optional.
- Using the card for emergencies. A card is not an alarm.
- Forgetting that cards cannot take photos. Link to a form or a SharePoint upload link with limited access.
- Free text without length limits, which makes the message too large.
- Treating 'Good practice' as noise. It is useful for the weekly SQDC board.

## Teams rendering limits and compatibility

- Teams rendering (Microsoft Learn cards reference, read 20 Nov 2026): bots and action-based message extensions support schema 1.6 or earlier, and the Teams mobile app up to 1.6; newer versions may render inconsistently. This card declares 1.5 on purpose, so it stays inside what older clients show.
- Adaptive Cards are not supported in tabs or connectors, and file or image uploads are not supported inside cards. Images must be PNG, JPEG or GIF up to 1024 x 1024 pixels, hosted at a public address without redirects; SVG and animated GIF are not supported.
- Design guidance from the same source: set wrap to true on text, avoid horizontal scrolling, use at most three columns per ColumnSet, give an explicit pixel width to at most one column and keep it small. Teams mobile does not scroll sideways.
- A card can have up to six actions, but one to three primary actions read best. The Learn page also lists positive and destructive action styling as not supported in some contexts, so the button titles must carry the meaning (check what your clients show).
- The whole message, including the card, is limited to about 28 KB (Teams connector page); keep cards small.
- Input.ChoiceSet with style compact shows a drop down, expanded shows radio buttons; both work on Teams desktop and mobile. Test the placeholder text on your clients.
- Action.OpenUrl inside an ActionSet is placed under the form, before the real actions.

## Adapt it

Add your categories (for example housekeeping, PPE, ergonomics, chemicals), add a location picker from your site map as a ChoiceSet, or add a severity choice. Keep the card to about eight fields.

## Privacy and security

A safety observation can contain personal data and, indirectly, accusations. Agree with your data protection officer and works council: who may read entries, how long they are kept, whether identity is ever stored. Limit access to the list to the safety officers. Do not use the data to rank individuals.

When a supplier is late, the buyer needs the facts side by side: how late, what is left in stock, whether another source exists, and what the next step is. This card shows exactly that and records the decision with one Action.Execute. It is meant for a bot or an agent that watches open purchase orders, for example one fed from your ERP or from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management. Microsoft describes a Supplier Communications Agent in that product that drafts reminder mails for a buyer to review (preview documentation, read 20 Nov 2026, may change); this card works independently of it. All values are invented examples.

