## How to use

1. Let your bot (or a flow that calls the bot) post the card when an order line has a confirmed date in the past and no goods receipt. Fill the facts from your ERP data. Compute 'Days late' and 'Stock covers' on the server; the card only shows them.
2. Handle adaptiveCard/action with verb supplierDelayDecision. Read po and line from data, newDate and next and note from the inputs. Answer with a replacement card without the inputs: 'Decision recorded by [buyer role]: send reminder draft'.
3. For next = reminder, create a mail draft, not a sent mail. A buyer or a second person reads the draft before it goes out. The draft states the order number, item, quantity, confirmed date and asks for a new confirmed date. Do not promise penalties or contract positions in the text.
4. For next = alt, create a task for the buyer to check the alternative source. For accept, write the new date into your tracking list and set a follow-up check. The ERP is still where dates and orders change; the card only records the intent.

## Worked example (invented data)

Confirmed date 2026-11-17, today 2026-11-20, so 3 days late. Needed by 2026-11-24, stock covers 2 working days: Friday 20th and Monday 23rd. The buyer enters 2026-11-25 as the supplier's new date, sees that stock ends before that, and selects 'Check the alternative source'. The bot creates a task and answers with a read-only card. If the stock had covered 6 working days, 'Accept the new date' would be reasonable.

## Common mistakes

- Sending mails automatically from the card. Create drafts and keep a human decision.
- Putting prices, contract terms or negotiation positions into the card; chat history is not a controlled record.
- Letting the client calculate days late or stock cover. Calculate on the server from ERP data.
- Mixing Action.Submit and Action.Execute. Use Execute for the whole card.
- Two ColumnSets with three or more columns each. Keep to two so the phone layout stays readable.

## Teams rendering limits and compatibility

- Teams rendering (Microsoft Learn cards reference, read 20 Nov 2026): bots and action-based message extensions support schema 1.6 or earlier, and the Teams mobile app up to 1.6; newer versions may render inconsistently. This card declares 1.5 on purpose, so it stays inside what older clients show.
- Adaptive Cards are not supported in tabs or connectors, and file or image uploads are not supported inside cards. Images must be PNG, JPEG or GIF up to 1024 x 1024 pixels, hosted at a public address without redirects; SVG and animated GIF are not supported.
- Design guidance from the same source: set wrap to true on text, avoid horizontal scrolling, use at most three columns per ColumnSet, give an explicit pixel width to at most one column and keep it small. Teams mobile does not scroll sideways.
- A card can have up to six actions, but one to three primary actions read best. The Learn page also lists positive and destructive action styling as not supported in some contexts, so the button titles must carry the meaning (check what your clients show).
- The whole message, including the card, is limited to about 28 KB (Teams connector page); keep cards small.
- The two-column FactSet layout is fine on Teams desktop. On Teams mobile columns can wrap or shrink, so keep fact titles short and test on a phone.
- Input.Date shows a date picker on all Teams clients; validate that the date is after today on the server.
- Action.Execute needs schema 1.4 or higher and a bot that handles the invoke.

## Adapt it

Add a Supplier quality score line from your own data (not invented), a link to the delivery history, or a third column for transport details. If you do not have a bot, build the same layout with Action.OpenUrl buttons that open the order in the ERP and a pre-filled mail draft link.

## Privacy and security

Supplier contacts are personal data; do not show names or phone numbers in the card unless needed. Keep the card within your company's confidentiality rules and out of channels with external guests.
